{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Count Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1174 he joined the Council of the Barons in Jerusalem and supported the claim of Guy of Lusignan to the throne. Raymond III was a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century. He married Eschiva de Bures the heiress of the Principality of Galilee in 1176 which strengthened his position.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a major role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he commanded a contingent of troops that contributed to the victory over Saladin. He was also involved in the failed invasion of Egypt in 1168 and the Battle of Jacob’s Ford in 1179. Raymond III acted as regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1185 to 1186. During his regency he worked to stabilize the kingdom amid internal strife and threats from Saladin. His policies often put him at odds with other nobles particularly the Ibelins and the Lusignans.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nIn 1186 Raymond III supported the claim of Humphrey IV of Toron against Guy of Lusignan for the throne of Jerusalem. When Humphrey submitted to Guy Raymond III refused to do the same and instead made a pact with Saladin. This alliance allowed Saladin’s troops to pass through his territory in 1187 which led to the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed. Raymond III barely escaped the battle and died later that year in Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s reign and actions remain controversial among historians. Some view him as a pragmatic leader trying to preserve the Crusader states while others see him as self-serving and willing to collaborate with the enemy to advance his own interests. His alliances and regencies shaped the politics of Jerusalem during a critical period. Raymond III died without a male heir and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV of Tripoli. His legacy is complex reflecting the turbulent times in which he lived.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.16611935198307037}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -0.9193849563598633}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0008784249657765031}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.14857740700244904}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -9.262132516596466e-05}, {"token": " page", "logprob": -0.4287737309932709}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -0.1940094530582428}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00026079590315930545}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -6.6756979322235566e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.785339014139026e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0025607910938560963}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.123234637314454e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0008345934911631048}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -6.174850568640977e-05}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.04413650184869766}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -3.4450891689630225e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.168443962931633}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.12375488132238388}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.529942543740617e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0005267662927508354}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.000996093382127583}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00013648532330989838}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0006333967321552336}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00015770144818816334}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.38703060150146484}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 